   "source": [
    "# Spark Architecture\n",
    "![](Images/ApacheSpark/Architecture%201.png)\n",
    "![](Images/ApacheSpark/Architecture%202.png)\n",
    "\n",
    "### Components:\n",
    "  1. **Cluster Manager’s Role(Resource Manager):** The cluster manager is responsible for provisioning resources, launching executors, and managing the worker infrastructure on which the Spark application runs.\n",
    "  Example: Spark's Own Standalone Cluster Manager, Mesos, Yarn and Kubernetes\n",
    "  2. **Spark Context(Spark Session):** The starting point of a Spark application. It connects the Driver node to the Cluster Manager and is used to request resources and manage the execution of Spark jobs.\n",
    "  3. **Driver Node:** The driver analyzes the Spark application, creates a logical plan, optimizes it into a physical plan, and breaks it down into stages and tasks. In databrick/Synapse/Fabric, no need to create session seperately when you attach the cluster it automatically creates the session.\n",
    "  ![](Images/ApacheSpark/DriverNode.png)\n",
    "  4. **Worker Node:** In Azure Databricks, there is typically a one-to-one relationship between worker nodes and executors, where each worker node runs a single executor. The executor processes tasks in parallel using multiple CPU cores.\n",
    "  However, PySpark UDFs introduce overhead because executors run on the JVM, while the UDF logic runs in the Python interpreter. This requires data transfer between the JVM and Python processes, which increases resource usage and can reduce performance in Apache Spark.\n",
    "  ![](Images/ApacheSpark/Worker%20Node.png)\n",
    "  5. **Notebooks and Applications:** In Databricks, notebooks are used to write Spark code. When the code is executed, it is submitted to the driver as a Spark application. Spark applications can also be submitted externally using jobs or spark-submit."
   ]
  },
