   "source": [
    "## Broadcast Join\n",
    "**How it works:** Spark takes the smaller table, sends a copy of it to every executor in the cluster, and stores it in memory. The larger table is then scanned, and the join happens locally on each node.\n",
    "\n",
    "**The Constraint:** By default, the small table must be under 10MB (controlled by spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold), though you can increase this or use the broadcast() hint.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Use Case:** Joining a massive Fact table (e.g., Transactions, 1TB) with a small Dimension table (e.g., Currency Codes or Country Names, 5MB).\n",
    "![](Images/ApacheSpark/BroadCast1.png)\n",
    "![](Images/ApacheSpark/BroadCast2.png)\n",
    "![](Images/ApacheSpark/BroadCast3.png)\n",
    "![](Images/ApacheSpark/BroadCast4.png)"
