   "source": [
    "### **Catalyst Optimizer(Spark SQL Engine)**\n",
    "#### Note: Catalyst = Planning, Not Execution(Catalyst performs logical and physical query planning. It does NOT execute queries and does NOT create RDDs. RDDs are created only after the selected physical plan is handed over to Spark Core.)\n",
    "![](Images/ApacheSpark/Catalyst%20Optimizer.png)\n",
    "\n",
    "##### **The Inputs (SQL Query, DataFrame, DataSet)**\n",
    "This is where the user starts. You can interact with Spark in three main ways:\n",
    "  SQL Query: Writing standard SQL code (e.g., SELECT * FROM table).\n",
    "  DataFrame: Using the untyped API (common in Python/PySpark).\n",
    "  DataSet: Using the strongly typed API (common in Scala/Java). Regardless of which one you use, Spark treats them all the same internally.\n",
    "\n",
    "##### **Unresolved Logical Plan**\n",
    "When you first run your code, Spark creates an \"Unresolved Logical Plan.\"\n",
    "Why is it unresolved? Spark understands the syntax of your query (it knows you want to FILTER or SELECT), but it doesn't know if the tables or column names you typed actually exist yet. It hasn't checked for errors.\n",
    "\n",
    "##### **Catalog**\n",
    "The Catalog is the internal repository of metadata (information about data).\n",
    "It holds the list of all tables, DataFrames, columns, and data types available.\n",
    "The optimizer consults the Catalog to verify that the tables and columns requested in the \"Unresolved Plan\" actually exist.\n",
    "\n",
    "##### **Logical Plan (Resolved)**\n",
    "Once the Unresolved Plan is checked against the Catalog, it becomes the Logical Plan.\n",
    "\"Resolve Attributes\": At this stage, if you typed a column name wrong, the code would fail. If it passes, the plan is now valid code, but it isn't optimized for speed yet.\n",
    "\n",
    "##### **Optimized Logical Plan**\n",
    "This is where the Catalyst Optimizer applies rule-based optimizations to make the logic more efficient without changing the result. The image lists three key optimizations:\n",
    "-   **Constant Folding:** Simplifying math immediately (e.g., changing x = 10 + 5 to x = 15 so the computer doesn't have to calculate it for every row).\n",
    "-   **Predicate Pushdown:** Moving filters as close to the data source as possible. (e.g., Filtering data before loading it into memory rather than loading it all and then filtering).\n",
    "-   **Projection Pruning:** Only reading the specific columns needed for the query, ignoring the rest to save I/O.\n",
    "\n",
    "##### Physical Plans & Cost Models\n",
    "Now Spark needs to decide how to actually run this logic on the cluster.\n",
    "-   **Physical Plans:** Spark generates several possible strategies for how to execute the query (e.g., \"Should I do a Broadcast Join or a Sort Merge Join?\").\n",
    "-   **Cost Models:** Spark simulates the cost of execution for each plan based on data statistics (volume of data, CPU usage, I/O cost). It compares the options to find the cheapest (fastest) one.\n",
    "\n",
    "##### Selected Physical Plan\n",
    "This is the \"winner\" of the cost comparison. It is the specific set of instructions on how to conduct the computation.\n",
    "As noted in the image, this decides things like: ParquetScan (how to read the file), Sort Merge Join (how to combine tables), Filter, Project, etc.\n",
    "![](Images/ApacheSpark/Plan.jpg)"
   ]
  },
