   "source": [
    "## Shuffle Joins\n",
    "![](Images/ApacheSpark/ShJ1.png)\n",
    "![](Images/ApacheSpark/ShJ2.png)\n",
    "![](Images/ApacheSpark/Join.png)\n",
    "![](Images/ApacheSpark/ShJ3.png)\n",
    "\n",
    "###### Sort Merge Join\n",
    "This is the default join strategy in Spark for large datasets.\n",
    "- **How it works:** It involves three distinct phases:\n",
    "- Shuffle: Both datasets are repartitioned based on the join key so that rows with the same key end up on the same executor.\n",
    "- Sort: Data within each partition is sorted by the join key.\n",
    "- Merge: Spark iterates through the sorted records and joins them (very memory efficient).\n",
    "- The Constraint: High overhead due to disk I/O and network transfer during the shuffle.\n",
    "- Use Case: Joining two very large tables (e.g., Orders and Users) where neither table can fit into an executor's memory.\n",
    "![](Images/ApacheSpark/SortMergeJoin.png)\n",
    "\n",
    "##### Shuffle Hash Join (SHJ)\n",
    "**How it works:** Like SMJ, it shuffles the data. However, instead of sorting the data, it builds a local hash table of the smaller partition.\n",
    "**When it's used:** Spark chooses this when one table is considerably smaller than the other (but still too big to broadcast) and the data is not already sorted.\n",
    "**Use Case:** When you have a moderately large table (e.g., 200MB) joining a huge table. It's faster than SMJ because it skips the \"Sort\" phase, but riskier because the hash table must fit in memory or you'll get OOM (Out of Memory) errors.\n",
    "![](Images/ApacheSpark/HashJoin.png)\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "application/vnd.databricks.v1+cell": {
